WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.625
CARTER MORGAN: Welcome to "Cloud
Security Basics," a new series

00:00:02.625 --> 00:00:04.800
where we explain the
ins and outs of securing

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.780
your application
on Google Cloud.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:07.350
Sound fun?

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.310
Then stick around,
because in this episode,

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.640
you'll learn who's
responsible for securing what

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:12.957
in your Cloud app.

00:00:12.957 --> 00:00:19.418
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:26.873 --> 00:00:27.867
[DIALING]

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.800
BRAD ACTOR: Hello,
Cloud Detective.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.050
Today I'm not issuing a
challenge-- only a riddle

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:37.050
for you.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.080
What do you have to do
to secure your cloud

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:40.450
environment by yourself?

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:44.930
CARTER MORGAN: OK,
here's what we know.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:47.330
When it comes to securing
their cloud environment,

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:49.970
enterprise companies
care about a few things--

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:51.740
consistently delivering
the right service

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:53.512
and the right data to
the right identity.

00:00:53.512 --> 00:00:55.970
This can't be something that
only happens some of the time.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:58.940
It needs to happen for
every single request.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.400
Implementing authorization
and authentication

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.459
to make sure that only the right
people have access to resources

00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:08.610
and data, proactively preventing
threats because bad actors

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.040
are constantly evolving,
and effective cloud security

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.790
allows you to be proactive
rather than just reactive.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.380
Complying with
regulatory requirements

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.713
of their industry-- not
all, but more and more,

00:01:18.713 --> 00:01:20.380
businesses are required
to be compliant,

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.576
so that's something you
have to be aware of.

00:01:22.576 --> 00:01:25.140
And flexibility and
control so internal teams

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.640
can be empowered to act on the
parts they're responsible for

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.290
and can control.

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:31.040
To make sure those
areas are covered,

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.096
we must harden our
cloud security.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.970
Securing your system falls
into three main levels

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.380
of responsibility--

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.690
things you're responsible
for, things your provider

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.960
is responsible for, and things
you're both responsible for.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.520
There's platform,
infrastructure,

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.627
and application-level security.

00:01:49.627 --> 00:01:51.960
The user is responsible for
securing their applications,

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.760
like setting up proper
authentication, authorization,

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:57.480
and identification for
users in their system.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.730
Google Cloud owns securing
the platform, which

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.200
includes managing the physical
machines and data centers,

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.090
your application, and data use.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.550
Lastly, infrastructure security
is a shared responsibility,

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:09.000
where Google Cloud
provides tools

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.060
to help the user manage their
VMs, network, and data access

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.390
needs.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:16.290
Let's explore this in depth.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:19.160
So what do you have to
do to secure your cloud

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.115
environment by yourself?

00:02:21.115 --> 00:02:22.550
Hm.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:24.840
I know that within these
levels of responsibilities,

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.715
there are three main
security actions you can

00:02:26.715 --> 00:02:28.450
take to harden your system--

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.190
platform and infra actions,
preventative actions,

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:33.170
and forensic actions.

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:35.530
Let me explain.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.060
Infrastructure and
platform actions

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:39.670
are steps you take to make
sure the underlying hardware,

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.670
or virtual hardware, are secure.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:44.200
There are two
components of this--

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.240
platform security, which
Google completely manages,

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.330
and infrastructure security,
which is managed by the user,

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:53.460
but Google Cloud
provides tools to help.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.020
Platform actions
are steps Google

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:56.850
takes to secure the
hardware and software

00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:00.077
tools available over the
internet they provide to users.

00:03:00.077 --> 00:03:02.160
This can be anything from
physically securing data

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.500
centers to replicating
data across regions

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.060
to make sure it's
available, even

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.520
in the event of a natural
disaster somewhere.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.360
Infrastructure actions,
on the other hand,

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.080
are steps the user takes
to secure the Cloud-based,

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.330
pay-as-you-go services such
as storage, networking,

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.948
and virtualization
that Google provides.

00:03:19.948 --> 00:03:22.240
This could be anything from
changing settings in a load

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.480
balancer to selecting a
more secure VM instance

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:27.790
type for your application.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.308
The next class of action
is preventative actions.

00:03:30.308 --> 00:03:32.100
These are steps you
take to avoid breaches,

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.440
like locking down
access controls.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.540
Much of this happens at
the application level

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.000
and includes things like Google
Cloud Identity and Access

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.590
Management, or IAM, and Google
Identity Aware Proxy, or IAP,

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.960
to limit who has access to
what inside of your system.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.470
So Google provides
tooling to the users

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:51.880
to help define access levels.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:53.930
And then there are
forensic actions.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:56.430
These are the steps you take
to track down breaches and stop

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:59.310
them quickly, or, ideally,
to identify and stop

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:02.700
malicious behaviors before
a breach ever even occurs.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.210
One of the main practices here
is logging and monitoring.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.960
By logging activity
within your system

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.620
and setting up automatic and
manual monitoring of that data,

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:13.950
you can be alerted of suspicious
activity before it's too late.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:15.750
Here, again, Google
Cloud provides

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:19.640
tooling to help customers
monitor their environments.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:20.579
That's it.

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:24.422
That's the answer
to Brad's question.

00:04:24.422 --> 00:04:25.630
BRAD ACTOR: Hello, Detective.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:27.550
I see you solved
my little riddle--

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:29.470
what do you have to do
to secure your cloud

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:32.410
environment by yourself?

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:34.090
I said by yourself.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:34.960
That was tricky.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:36.520
But here's the thing--

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:38.860
you were never alone in this.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:42.470
Cloud security is a shared
responsibility model.

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:45.210
Google handles most of the
infrastructure security.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:47.920
But you are ultimately
responsible for securing

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.420
your applications
and services that

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:52.300
run on that infrastructure.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:54.400
You can never assume an
application or service

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.350
is 100% secure.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:58.090
But by leveraging
tools that focus

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:00.670
on detection and by
planning for recovery,

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:03.130
you're ensuring your
security model has a chance

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:05.380
at keeping your service safe.

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:07.570
Very good, very good.

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:09.490
You've solved my little
riddle this time.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:10.660
But be ready.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:12.220
Because next time,
I want to see what

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:16.078
you'll do if the wrong
identities access your data.

00:05:16.078 --> 00:05:18.370
CARTER MORGAN: So there you
have it-- the first episode

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:20.170
of "Cloud Security Basics."

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:23.200
Next episode will focus on the
first of three distinct areas

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:24.750
of cloud security risk--

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:25.885
access.

00:05:25.885 --> 00:05:27.760
In the meantime, if you
want to take a deeper

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.493
dive into the cloud
security model,

00:05:29.493 --> 00:05:31.660
then check out the article
linked in the description

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:32.183
below.

00:05:32.183 --> 00:05:34.600
And stay tuned for the rest
of the "Cloud Security Basics"

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.950
series, because when
it comes to security,

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:38.560
you can't let bad actors win.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.710
[THEME MUSIC]

